{
  "dom_id": "step-q351057",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=4{sec}^{2}\u2061t,$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4{sec}^{2}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(0)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =0+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}+4\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+4(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
